      T([`Property`, `Explanation`], [[`Simple and unambiguous`, `A one-hot vector uniquely identifies a vocabulary item`], [`Very high dimensional`, `A vocabulary of 50,000 words gives vectors with 50,000 dimensions`], [`No semantic similarity`, `cat and dog are as orthogonal as cat and movie; the geometry does not encode relatedness`], [`Useful as an internal index`, `Neural models start from token IDs, then map them to trainable embedding vectors`]]),
      W(`Problem: the space treats all different words as equally unrelated. With cat = [1,0,0], dog = [0,1,0], movie = [0,0,1], every dot product between different words is 0. Neural models still use this identity: the token ID selects a row of an embedding matrix, and that dense row is what the model actually uses.`, `المشكلة: الفضاء يعامل كل الكلمات المختلفة كأنها غير مترابطة بالتساوي، فحاصل الضرب النقطي بين أي كلمتين مختلفتين = 0. ومع ذلك تستخدم النماذج العصبية هذه الهوية: الرقم يختار صفًّا من مصفوفة التضمين.`)
